            <ArticleTitle>Gamma-aminobutyric acid amides of nortriptyline and fluoxetine display improved pain suppressing activity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3010-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/jm900143u</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The GABA amides of the antidepressants nortriptyline and fluoxetine, 1 and 2, were compared to their respective parent compounds in rodent models of pain. The amides significantly reduced early nociceptive and late inflammatory responses compared to nortriptyline or fluoxetine, where 1 exhibited overall better efficacy than 2. Amide 1 was most efficacious in lowering cytokine secretion, edema and hyperalgesia induced by formalin and lambda-carrageenan, respectively. Thus, 1 is a promising candidate for the treatment of pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
